In the field of network programming, developers frequently utilize socket programming to establish reliable communication between distinct computing systems. A standard baseline for testing these network connections is an "echo" client-server model. In this architecture, a client system transmits a text string to a server system over a network; the server processes the incoming data and returns the exact string to the client, confirming bilateral connectivity. 

Below is an objective, functional overview of how this communication is established using the Java programming language across two different systems on the same local area network (LAN).

