By default, Unity's built-in `[Header("Text")]` attribute does not support collapsing. However, there are three highly effective ways to achieve collapsible sections in the Unity Inspector to keep your code organized and clean.

Here are the best methods, ranging from native workarounds to popular plugins:

### Method 1: The Native Unity Way (Using `[Serializable]` Structs)
If you don't want to install any plugins or write custom editor scripts, the easiest way to create a collapsible section is to group your variables inside a custom `struct` or `class` and mark it as `[System.Serializable]`. Unity automatically draws these as collapsible foldouts!

**Example:**
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    // This will create a collapsible header named "Movement Settings"
    [System.Serializable]
    public struct MovementSettings
    {
        public float walkSpeed;
        public float runSpeed;
        public float jumpForce;
    }

    // This will create a collapsible header named "Combat Settings"
    [System.Serializable]
    public struct CombatSettings
    {
        public int maxHealth;
        public int damage;
    }

    public MovementSettings movement;
    public CombatSettings combat;
}
```

### Method 2: The Best Free Plugin ("NaughtyAttributes")
If you want to keep your variables loose in the main class but still want collapsible headers, the open-source community asset **NaughtyAttributes** is the best free solution. It adds a `[Foldout]` attribute.

1. Download **NaughtyAttributes** from the Unity Asset Store (it's free).
2. Add `using NaughtyAttributes;` to the top of your script.
3. Use the `[Foldout("Name")]` attribute.

**Example:**
```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using NaughtyAttributes;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [Foldout("Movement Settings")]
    public float walkSpeed;

    [Foldout("Movement Settings")]
    public float jumpForce;

    [Foldout("Combat Settings")]
    public int health;
}
```

### Method 3: The Industry Standard Premium Plugin ("Odin Inspector")
If you are working on a larger commercial project, almost all professional Unity studios use an asset called **Odin Inspector**. It completely overhauls the Unity Inspector and provides massive customization, including foldouts.

If you have Odin, you can use `[FoldoutGroup]`:

**Example:**
```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using Sirenix.OdinInspector;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [FoldoutGroup("Movement Settings")]
    public float walkSpeed;

    [FoldoutGroup("Movement Settings")]
    public float jumpForce;
}
```

